#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078906 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36524faf-ac61-5756-89e3-c83a947ac61b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Closed Intervals of Continuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92d536f4-f3c9-5a23-8406-3085c71f3896" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[- 3, 0]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076648} {:answer/id #uuid "b7ccc3f7-809c-557f-a346-9e152fa758d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[0, 4]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076649} {:answer/id #uuid "e0d64979-2b51-5dd3-b0b1-e8eb32c8d31d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[- 2, 2]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076650} {:answer/id #uuid "1a1a8a7e-e736-596f-b233-8ebe095a951a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[0, 1]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076651} {:answer/id #uuid "64faa4e5-f941-533c-8377-e9cf201530db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[- 1, 1]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076652}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "92d536f4-f3c9-5a23-8406-3085c71f3896" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[- 3, 0]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076648} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8883715-fa77-5aeb-a79b-23968acc0726" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076647}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c3dac14f-cad9-599e-9b64-bbf71b23d459" :question/math-academy-id "q-48238" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/assets/3027743251b98968805564e296d7570d80d35ff64b27cf831e631ed9c8efb40a.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $y=f(x)$ above continuous?" :question/worked-solution "The function is continuous on the open intervals $(-3,0)$ and $(0,\\infty ).$\n\nAdditionally, the function is right-continuous at $x=-3$ and left-continuous at $x=0.$ Hence, it is continuous on the closed interval $[-3,0].$\n\nTherefore, we can say that the function is continuous on the intervals $[-3,0]$ and $(0,\\infty ).$ It is also continuous on any subinterval of these intervals.\n\nAmong the answer choices, the correct one is the following:\n\n$\\,$ $[-3,0]$ \n\nNotice that:\n\n- $[0,1]$ and $[0,4]$ contain the endpoint $x=0,$ but the function is not right-continuous at this point.\n\n- $[-1,1]$ and $[-2,2]$ contain the point of discontinuity $x=0,$ so the function is not continuous on these intervals."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300034 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbfddefc-d68b-5b1e-9ec5-fd18be204d92" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Semi-Open Intervals of Continuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96ea7e20-13e0-5802-a213-2cdefded01e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/assets/3c24d4207dc777027919d211e4b2262d7340caf25a4166fa004e6e8129da5614.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300036} {:answer/id #uuid "6bb18bc0-9a99-5855-b7c6-0f74a53c2b27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/assets/387ed50e8983c83f96ea03bdd7a47d13d33a0c6e0728b52512fef8296351f73e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300037} {:answer/id #uuid "71a8cbbb-31f6-54c3-8cc7-59c26a538c4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/assets/895c64a995d7f07a161a43945c369395b5cb365c5e7ec0f664df130c49795af8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300038} {:answer/id #uuid "4bd931eb-aff4-51b2-bf97-e00df57b9670" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/assets/7f2b4da10350a6ba230e0ec1b0b26822b133ee4a9c1228a61565ed559efcdd64.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300039} {:answer/id #uuid "45008099-0a9a-5bef-82a2-0f66e7d2dce8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/assets/3c5fedec0f96ef81c33146504cdabc8f0400ba79f77591167a285d119d238fd2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300040}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96ea7e20-13e0-5802-a213-2cdefded01e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/assets/3c24d4207dc777027919d211e4b2262d7340caf25a4166fa004e6e8129da5614.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300036} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4de47f3-3670-52cc-9337-ef541d36c72a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300035}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0790ffcc-e651-53c9-a00a-a9cbd12d21cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-48279" :question/problem "Which of the following functions is continuous on the interval $(1,3]?$" :question/worked-solution "The correct answer is the following.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/assets/3c24d4207dc777027919d211e4b2262d7340caf25a4166fa004e6e8129da5614.png)\n\nIndeed, we have that $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval $(1,3].$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- $f(x)$, $h(x)$,  and $q(x)$ are not right-continuous at $x=3.$\n\n- $p(x)$ is not continuous at  $x=2$, and so is not continuous on $(1,3].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078909 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f00fd821-a5d4-5844-9db5-795b9e321b94" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Largest Intervals of Continuity of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "add6e0c1-de65-521d-ac54-d9f9aa79e22a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function is not continuous on any of the given intervals" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076666} {:answer/id #uuid "ed4108d3-d503-5380-9b91-e52d70a2c732" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076667} {:answer/id #uuid "0761dc29-c65e-51af-93e5-5764d3dd1208" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076668} {:answer/id #uuid "2806df36-b017-577f-a98c-fe102b72369a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076669} {:answer/id #uuid "46321205-9864-5a4e-9154-e1874685c5c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076670}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0761dc29-c65e-51af-93e5-5764d3dd1208" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076668} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8a2e2c5-e973-5b38-97f5-2cdcf6261f36" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076665}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "183472b0-16c2-5809-99fd-33beade724a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-48344" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/assets/09d006953b4fc1d111a84e78fcc6b2038d53768fb82d6f61a0b2a1d0126bbb40.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ above. On which of the following intervals is the function continuous? \n\n- $x\\in (-\\infty ,0]$\n- $x\\in [-3,2]$\n- $x\\in (0,\\infty )$\n\nYou may assume that all points of discontinuity of $f(x)$ are shown in the diagram." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement.\n\n- The function is *not* continuous on $(-\\infty ,0]$ because it contains a discontinuity at $x=-3.$\n\n- The function is continuous on $[-3,2].$ It is continuous on $(-3,2),$ and also right continuous at $x=-3$ and left continuous at $x=2.$\n\n- The function is *not* continuous on $(0,\\infty )$ because it contains a discontinuity at $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300041 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e806aac-62fc-5c53-a15e-a2ce175dad25" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Open Intervals of Continuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a7caed5-7e4a-5703-af86-fc3c066955ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/assets/27cc3b76baa60c48f103f7d016bb78716330dbb03be791458489e5458bf326bd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300043} {:answer/id #uuid "6b2ea36d-5419-51be-8d6d-b59e1d3029df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/assets/c6cfdc41036d1dc319054c0cf397276f40faa2c749ea59820b45875cb31b3d9f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300044} {:answer/id #uuid "cc44ad77-478a-5e1b-87ae-4441934bc6f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/assets/59bc174fabd3f93321505a54f05832fda46914c8f8f8ad5e88690f53bef3e845.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300045} {:answer/id #uuid "0716dac2-625d-56c5-8c47-9d76752261b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/assets/c56ea94b0d5102c276d7cd4a196517a41611e3f7da82b3ab13c6b404c31cf430.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300046} {:answer/id #uuid "156b1b5f-b46f-5968-8e56-f0a0670df8b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/assets/47dca98b1ac1c7d3310418c3d5eaf58416827aaa5d97d118aa5f3b4a6d59d9f8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300047}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b2ea36d-5419-51be-8d6d-b59e1d3029df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/assets/c6cfdc41036d1dc319054c0cf397276f40faa2c749ea59820b45875cb31b3d9f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300044} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2565675-69c4-5459-9308-0049091ec89f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300042}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b8055c33-18c1-54b4-a55d-b97e3d2f96f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-76091" :question/problem "Which of the following functions is continuous on the interval $(-2,\\infty )?$ You may assume that all points of discontinuity for each function are shown." :question/worked-solution "The correct answer is the following.\n\n  \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/assets/c6cfdc41036d1dc319054c0cf397276f40faa2c749ea59820b45875cb31b3d9f.png)\n\n    \n\nIndeed, we have that $h(x)$ is continuous on the open interval $(-2,\\infty ).$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=0,$ but this point lies within the interval $(-2,\\infty ),$\n\n- $q(x)$ and $p(x)$ are not continuous at $x=1,$ but this point lies within the interval $(-2,\\infty ),$\n\n- $g(x)$is not continuous at $x=2,$ but this point lies within the interval $(-2,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300048 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f00fd821-a5d4-5844-9db5-795b9e321b94" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Largest Intervals of Continuity of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d73ccb5f-3a85-59e2-9885-dcbf6e34c870" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300050} {:answer/id #uuid "f4d38868-6ee5-58f5-aca1-4b5445eafad0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function is not continuous on any of the given intervals" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300051} {:answer/id #uuid "c54bdda8-c62c-5bda-b721-accc2417173c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300052} {:answer/id #uuid "13d7c789-e62f-53d2-ab93-14153ebbe1f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300053} {:answer/id #uuid "3252e2e0-1955-5227-ad8f-abd15b84a594" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300054}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c54bdda8-c62c-5bda-b721-accc2417173c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300052} :answer-field/id #uuid "cef2f2a6-04a0-5767-81fd-e969532c0d69" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300049}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "70359685-3e4e-53a5-8fbd-53c281152289" :question/math-academy-id "q-76210" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/assets/5e787dda0cccdafaa6a93176daea5f954b1cd1da0beaff410a6563536459208e.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ above. On which of the following intervals is the function continuous? \n\n- $x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]$\n- $x\\in [-2,0]$\n- $x\\in [-1,\\infty )$\n\nYou may assume that all points of discontinuity of $f(x)$ are shown in the diagram." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement.\n\n- The function is continuous on $(-\\infty ,-1].$ It is continuous on $(-\\infty ,-1)$ and also left continuous at $x=-1.$\n\n- The function is *not* continuous on $[-2,0]$ because it contains a discontinuity at $x=-1.$\n\n- The function is *not* continuous on $[-1,\\infty ).$ Although the function is continuous on $(-1,\\infty ),$ it is not right-continuous at $x=-1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}]}
